// =============================================================================
// appraisal-course — "Appraisal: What a Thing Is Actually Worth"
// =============================================================================
// Course TWELVE of the Real Estate department. Eleven courses covered the
// ground, the stewardship, the transaction, the tenant, the building, the
// partner, the lender, the record, the loss, the looking and the hardest hour.
// This one covers the number every one of them runs on and none of them
// examines: WHAT A THING IS ACTUALLY WORTH, and who says so.
//
// THE ARC, one argument in eight moves:
//   1. Thy estimation                    (Leviticus 27:2-8)
//   2. Whether it be good or bad         (Leviticus 27:14)
//   3. According to the years that remain (Leviticus 27:16-18, 23, 24)
//   4. He shall add the fifth part       (Leviticus 27:15, 19, 20, 21, 31)
//   5. Twenty gerahs shall be the shekel (Leviticus 27:25; Ezekiel 45:9-12)
//   6. The balances of deceit            (Hosea 12:7)
//   7. The worth of it in money          (1 Kings 21:1, 2, 15, 16)
//   8. What profit shall this birthright do to me (Genesis 25:29-34;
//                                          Jeremiah 32:25; Isaiah 55:1-2)
//
// Get a published schedule that everybody can see in advance, with a poverty
// override written into it -> get one binding estimate from a disinterested
// party -> learn that the value of land is the HARVESTS REMAINING rather than
// the dirt -> get a fixed addition that removes the incentive to game the number
// -> fix the UNIT before valuing anything, and watch a prophet re-fix the same
// unit centuries later -> see the short sharp verdict on a man who loves the
// crooked scale -> find out that a genuinely FAIR offer can still be wrong ->
// and end on the appraiser's own condition, because a faint man priced an
// inheritance at one meal.
//
// THE FIND THIS COURSE IS BUILT ON. Leviticus 27 is a COMPLETE VALUATION
// STATUTE, and it carries every element a modern appraisal practice has plus
// two most of them lack:
//
//   * a PUBLISHED SCHEDULE, knowable in advance by everyone (27:3-7)
//   * an ABILITY-TO-PAY OVERRIDE that sets the schedule aside entirely --
//     "according to his ability that vowed shall the priest value him" (27:8)
//   * a DISINTERESTED VALUER whose single number BINDS -- "as the priest shall
//     estimate it, so shall it stand" (27:14). No shopping for a second figure.
//   * valuation by PRODUCTIVE CAPACITY rather than by area: a field is priced
//     by the seed it takes (27:16)
//   * a PRO-RATION by remaining term -- "according to the years that remain,
//     even unto the year of the jubile, and it shall be abated" (27:18). The
//     value of land is the harvests you will actually get before it reverts.
//   * a FIXED REDEMPTION PREMIUM, uniform and stated: add the fifth part
//     (27:15, 19, 31). Twenty per cent, every time, so nobody has a reason to
//     lean on the valuer.
//   * an EXPIRY with no reversion (27:20, 21)
//   * and the UNIT DEFINED in the same chapter -- "twenty gerahs shall be the
//     shekel" (27:25)
//
// THE FOUR LIMITS THIS COURSE HOLDS, each a place the material is easily
// abused, and each pinned by the course test rather than trusted to prose:
//
//   Leviticus 27:2-7 -- this is the money equivalent of a VOW, never a
//     statement of what a person is worth. The whole passage is about
//     redeeming a person who was dedicated by a vow, and verse 8 sets the
//     entire schedule aside for a poor man, which no valuation of intrinsic
//     worth would ever do. It must NEVER be taught as Yahweh pricing people,
//     and never as a statement about men, women, the young or the old.
//   Leviticus 27:14 against 27:33 -- the same chapter says "whether it be good
//     or bad" TWICE, with opposite force. The priest DOES estimate a house
//     good or bad (27:14); the tithe of the herd is NOT searched good or bad
//     and may not be swapped (27:33). The difference is whose the thing is.
//     Flattening the two into one principle inverts one of them.
//   1 Kings 21 -- Ahab's offer was genuinely fair. A better vineyard, or the
//     worth of it in money. It was still wrong, because "what is it worth" is
//     downstream of "is it for sale," and Naboth answered about an inheritance
//     rather than a price. This is never a teaching that fair offers are wrong.
//   Genesis 25 -- the text renders its own verdict, "thus Esau despised his
//     birthright" (25:34), so this lesson does not invent one. And it is not a
//     comment on Jacob's conduct, on which this passage says nothing here. We
//     teach what it shows -- a faint man valuing an inheritance at one meal --
//     and stop where it stops.
//
// FRESHNESS, MEASURED BEFORE AUTHORING (DR-0076 sec.4), and the measurement
// killed most of a planned course. The obvious appraisal anchors are all
// already mounted in this same department: Genesis 23 and Abraham weighing the
// silver is buying-terms; Jeremiah 32:6-15 and the sealed deed is
// property-principle; Proverbs 11:1 and Deuteronomy 25:13-16, the just weight,
// are buying-terms; Ruth 4 is property-principle; Luke 14:28 and the count to
// finish is property-principle; 2 Samuel 24:24 and the refusal to offer what
// cost nothing is insurance-risk; Micah 6:11 is buying-terms; and Haggai 1:6,
// the bag with holes, is maintenance-trades. Eight natural lessons died there.
// What survived is better, because it drove the course into Leviticus 27,
// which no one teaches and which turns out to be the whole statute.
//
// WHAT IS TRUE, MEASURED AGAINST THE MOUNTED FILE rather than against the plan
// (course eleven's first header claimed a verse count off its candidate pool
// and named a verse it did not cite; this one was measured after wiring):
//
//   * this course cites 34 verses
//   * ZERO of them are cited anywhere else in the Real Estate department
//   * ZERO of them are cited anywhere else in the CATALOG either, which is a
//     stronger result than course eleven managed and is stated because it was
//     measured, not because it was aimed at
//   * TWO chapters are shared with one other course in the department, at
//     different verses, and both are property-principle:
//       1 Kings 21  -- here :1, :2, :15, :16 (the offer, and what followed the
//                      refusal); property-principle at :3, :4, :19
//       Jeremiah 32 -- here :25 alone; property-principle at :10, :14, :15, :44
//
// THE NABOTH NARRATIVE IS SHARED ON PURPOSE, and it is named rather than
// glossed over. property-principle teaches Naboth from the refusal and Ahab's
// sulk; lesson seven here takes the OFFER and what happened after the refusal.
// Same account, different verses, a different question: that course asks what
// an inheritance is, this one asks why a fair price did not settle it. Lesson
// seven deliberately SUMMARISES Naboth's answer rather than quoting it, says
// out loud that it is summarising, and sends the reader to the verse -- which
// keeps the verse-level count at zero without hiding anything from the reader.
//
// BANDS, BENEFITS AND STORIES FROM THE FIRST COMMIT (DR-0509), every hard term
// leads with its plain meaning (DR-0521), and every quoted span is verbatim
// from the repository's own KJV with its own reference and no ellipsis
// (DR-0459). Our prose says Yahweh; quotations keep "God" and "the LORD"
// exactly as the KJV has them (DR-0210).
//
// TEACHING, NOT APPRAISAL, TAX OR INVESTMENT ADVICE -- carried on the meta.
// =============================================================================

import {
  buildScheduleFor, progressSummaryFor, exportCurriculumMarkdownFor,
} from './church-classes.js';

export const APPRAISAL_CARE_NOTE =
  'Teaching, not appraisal, tax or investment advice. What your building is actually worth today, what a lender will lend against it, what your county has assessed it at, how to appeal that assessment, and what a licensed appraiser is required to consider are specific to your property and your state, and they change. Nothing in this course produces a number for your house. What it gives you is the SHAPE of an honest valuation — who makes it, what it is measured against, what the unit is, and what a fair addition over it looks like — so that you can read a real appraisal and tell whether it was done straight. Hire somebody licensed in your own state for the number, and talk to a tax professional before you act on an assessed value. If somebody has already valued something of yours and you think it was wrong, start at lesson two and lesson five — those two are about who gets to make the estimate and what it is measured against.';

