        title: 'A Year Of Three Hundred And Sixty Days',
        body: 'I signed a note and I read it properly, which I want on the record because I do not always. Rate, term, payment, penalty, all of it. What I did not do was look up what the word year meant in that document, because I am a grown man and I know what a year is. It was three hundred and sixty days. Twelve months of thirty, which is an old convention and is not a trick and is disclosed in the definitions section that I had skimmed because definitions sections are where nothing happens. Over the life of that note it cost me somewhat more than I expected, in a way that never showed up in any number I had checked, because every number I had checked was correct. That is the part worth saying. Nobody lied to me. The rate was the rate. The term was the term. The unit was quietly not what I assumed, and the unit was the price the entire time, and I had read the document twice without reading the one page where the words are defined. I now read definitions first and figures second, and it looks eccentric to people, and it has caught two things in four years that would otherwise have caught me.',
