    lesson: 'Four lessons of figures, and now the question that goes unasked in almost every valuation anybody in the room has ever received: figures of WHAT? Look at how the chapter closes its valuation rules. "And all thy estimations shall be according to the shekel of the sanctuary: twenty gerahs shall be the shekel." (Leviticus 27:25) A gerah is the smallest weight in that system. The law does not merely publish a price of fifty shekels — it publishes what a shekel IS. Twenty gerahs, fixed, and not whatever stone a particular merchant happens to keep in his bag. Give the room the consequence before you move on, because everything after it depends on their feeling this one sentence: YOU MAY PUBLISH EVERY FIGURE IN THE KINGDOM, AND IF THE UNIT CAN MOVE YOU HAVE PUBLISHED NOTHING. Fifty shekels is fifty of something. Whoever controls what that something weighs controls the price, while the buyer stands there believing he is reading it. THE UNIT IS THE PRICE. Now the observation that makes this lesson worth an hour. Centuries later, speaking to a different generation in a different crisis, Ezekiel sets the same definition again — and he states it from the opposite end of the sentence. "And the shekel shall be twenty gerahs:" (Ezekiel 45:12) Leviticus: twenty gerahs shall be the shekel. Ezekiel: the shekel shall be twenty gerahs. The content is identical and the construction is mirrored. Tell the room to check that themselves rather than take it from you, because a teacher who is right about a small textual claim has earned something, and one who is loose about it has spent something. Note honestly, out loud, that the verse continues on to define a larger weight as well, which this course does not treat, so nobody leaves thinking the clause was the whole verse. Then ask the question that opens the lesson: WHY WOULD THIS EVER NEED SAYING TWICE? The answer is in the material Ezekiel says it inside of. "Thus saith the Lord GOD; Let it suffice you, O princes of Israel: remove violence and spoil, and execute judgment and justice, take away your exactions from my people, saith the Lord GOD." (Ezekiel 45:9) That is an indictment of rulers, and the charges are violence, spoil and exactions — what was being squeezed out of the people. And the sentence immediately after it is: "Ye shall have just balances, and a just ephah, and a just bath." (Ezekiel 45:10) An ephah is a dry measure; a bath is a liquid one. THE MEASURES HAD DRIFTED, and the drift is listed in the same breath as violence and extortion, because in its effect it is the same thing arriving by a quieter road. Now land the principle and give the room real time with it, because it will explain a good deal they have lived through and never been able to name. A MAN NEED NOT STEAL VISIBLY IF THE MEASURE QUIETLY SHRINKS. Nobody sees a shekel of nineteen gerahs. Nobody feels a basket five per cent smaller than last year’s. Every printed price holds steady, every party can point at an unchanged document, everybody is being robbed, and there is nothing to point at. That is why the unit gets fixed, and re-fixed, and why the re-fixing appears inside a charge against princes rather than in a manual of weights and measures. Ask the room for their own version of it and expect a long list: floor area measured by two conventions and labelled by neither, a year that is three hundred and sixty days, a rate quoted per month against one quoted per annum, the word net with the deductions unnamed, used against refurbished, a warranty year that starts at manufacture rather than at purchase. In every one of those, the printed number is honest and the argument is not about price at all. Finally, show them the design feature, because it is genuinely elegant and it is the practical instruction. "The ephah and the bath shall be of one measure, that the bath may contain the tenth part of an homer, and the ephah the tenth part of an homer: the measure thereof shall be after the homer." (Ezekiel 45:11) The dry measure and the liquid measure are deliberately built to the same proportion of the same larger measure, which means EACH CAN BE USED TO CHECK THE OTHER. That is not custom piling up over centuries. That is a system designed so it can be audited from the inside, by an ordinary person, without having to trust the man holding the vessel — which is the only kind of honesty that survives a generation of princes.',
