    lesson: 'Seven sessions on the method. This last one is about the man performing it, and it is the lesson that catches everybody in the room, including whoever is teaching it. Show them first how deliberately the text stages it. "And Jacob sod pottage: and Esau came from the field, and he was faint:" (Genesis 25:29) Sod is an old word for boiled; faint means weak with hunger. The narrator supplies the valuer’s CONDITION before anything at all is offered or agreed. Then the man supplies it himself: "And Esau said to Jacob, Feed me, I pray thee, with that same red pottage; for I am faint: therefore was his name called Edom." (Genesis 25:30) Twice in two verses, from two different voices. Scripture is making certain the reader knows what state this man is in before watching him value anything. THAT IS NOT SCENE-SETTING. IT IS THE ARGUMENT. Now give the room his reasoning and insist they take it seriously rather than treating it as folly, because treating it as folly is how everybody escapes the lesson: "And Esau said, Behold, I am at the point to die: and what profit shall this birthright do to me?" (Genesis 25:32) That is a valuation argument and it is a coherent one. Work it aloud with them. If a man genuinely is at the point of death, then a benefit maturing years hence really is worth nothing to him, and immediate sustenance really is worth everything, and the discount he applies is not merely defensible — it is correct. Wait for somebody in the room to try to find the flaw in his logic, because there is not one. THE ARITHMETIC WAS NEVER THE PROBLEM. THE INPUT WAS. He was not at the point to die. He was hungry, in from a day in the field, and hunger had told him something about his own circumstances that he believed and acted upon inside a few minutes. Now land the principle in the form a steward can use: A VALUATION MADE IN AN EXTREME STATE PRICES THE STATE, NOT THE ASSET. Then go round the room and have each person name the state that does their own arithmetic for them, and expect the answers to be far smaller and more ordinary than anybody anticipates — tiredness, a particular kind of insult, an unopened envelope, a conversation with a sibling that went badly that morning. Nobody’s state is dramatic. That is exactly why it is not adjusted for. Then let the Word render its own verdict and add nothing whatsoever to it: "Then Jacob gave Esau bread and pottage of lentiles; and he did eat and drink, and rose up, and went his way: thus Esau despised his birthright." (Genesis 25:34) DESPISED — not erred, not miscalculated. And make the room hear the pace of that sentence: he did eat, and drink, and rose up, and went his way. Four verbs, no pause, no reflection, the entire disposal of something permanent absorbed into the back half of an afternoon. Hold the limit plainly before anybody extends it: THIS PASSAGE IS NOT COMMENTING ON JACOB’S CONDUCT HERE, and this lesson does not either. What it shows is a man in a state, pricing something permanent against something immediate, and Scripture’s own summary of what that was. Now give them the inverse, because a course should not close on the failure. A man is instructed to buy a field in a city that is about to fall, and in prayer he states the difficulty out loud instead of smoothing it away: "And thou hast said unto me, O Lord GOD, Buy thee the field for money, and take witnesses; for the city is given into the hand of the Chaldeans." (Jeremiah 32:25) Every available signal said the asset was worthless. He bought it on Yahweh’s word, and he did not pretend the contradiction was not a contradiction — the honesty and the obedience are in the same sentence, which is a thing worth noticing on its own. Set the two men side by side and leave them there: ESAU SOLD SOMETHING LASTING TO RELIEVE SOMETHING IMMEDIATE. JEREMIAH BOUGHT SOMETHING LASTING WHILE EVERYTHING IMMEDIATE ARGUED AGAINST IT. Then close the whole course on a question and an offer. "Wherefore do ye spend money for that which is not bread? and your labour for that which satisfieth not? hearken diligently unto me, and eat ye that which is good, and let your soul delight itself in fatness." (Isaiah 55:2) Say it plainly: a man can run every method in these eight sessions perfectly — publish the schedule, use a disinterested valuer, compute the pro-ration, fix the added fifth, fix the unit, refuse the crooked balance, ask whether it is for sale, guard his own state — and still spend an entirely accurate number on something that is not bread. And then the offer, with its price named: "Ho, every one that thirsteth, come ye to the waters, and he that hath no money; come ye, buy, and eat; yea, come, buy wine and milk without money and without price." (Isaiah 55:1) Eight lessons on getting a number right, and the thing of highest worth in all of them carries no price at all, and is held out first to the man who has none.',
